  "metadata": {
    "title": "Apple to raise gadget prices as AI drives up costs",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Apple has warned it will have to raise prices because of soaring costs caused by the AI boom.\nTim Cook, Apple's chief executive, said higher prices were now \"unavoidable\" across its range of gadgets, claiming they would remain elevated until costs return to \"reasonable levels\".\nHe did not say what products would be affected, but the warning comes before the release of the upcoming iPhone 18, expected to be announced in September.\nPrice rises could push the cost well above £2,000 for the most expensive models.\n\"Unfortunately, price increases are unavoidable,\" Mr Cook told the Wall Street Journal.\n\"We're doing our best to mitigate the huge increases that are being passed to us, and we've been trying to shield our customers from the increases, but the situation has become unsustainable.\"\nThe threat of inflation stems from increased competition for AI memory chips.\nAI data centres require large amounts of high-bandwidth memory, and soaring demand has pushed manufacturers to focus on AI chips.\nThis has in turn led to a shortage of memory chips known as dynamic random access memory (Dram), which are used in smartphones, computers and games consoles.\nThis week, analysts at CCS Insight said smartphone sales were expected to fall by 15pc this year to the weakest level in a decade.\nThey said sales would fall again next year to the lowest level since 2013.\nMr Cook locked Apple's suppliers into long-term contracts that have so far protected the company from soaring memory costs. However, as these contracts lapse, the world's most valuable smartphone maker becomes increasingly exposed.\nResearch company Trendforce says contract Dram prices doubled in the first quarter of 2026 compared with three months earlier.\nThey now make up to a quarter of the cost of producing a phone.\nMr Cook had previously said the company has \"a range of options\" to navigate the memory crisis.\nThe current iPhone 17 costs between £799 and £1,999, depending on the model and how much storage it has.\nNew AI features in the latest models could require an increase in the onboard memory required by the phones.\nAnalysts have speculated that this could push the cost of the cheapest iPhone 18 up by at least £100.\nApple is also expected to unveil its first foldable phone in September. Mr Cook will step down as chief executive shortly before, on September 1.\nThe tech giant recently raised the price of its Mac Mini computer, which has become popular among hobbyists running AI agents on their home devices.",
    "text_blocks": [
